  "query_preview": "..\nVisible satellite imagery shows a developing cumulus field over\nthe higher terrain, but the development into showers and\nthunderstorms appears to be suppressed by the high over New\nMexico, despite the presence of the monsoonal moisture. Still\nexpecting isolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms to \ndevelop over the higher terrain later this afternoon, with storm \nmotion pushing convection toward the north-northeast today.\nConvection south of the Mogollon Rim is expected to be limited,\nmostly associated with any outflows that push activity southward.\n\nForecast guidance continues to support the high pressure\nweakening slightly Tuesday and Wednesday, before strengthening\nagain as it moves westward later this week. This may allow a\nslight increase in showers and thunderstorms Tueday and Wednesday,\nthought the greatest chance remains over and near the higher\nterrain. As the high strengthens and shifts westward later this \nweek, temperatures will begin to climb Thursday and continuing \ninto the weekend. Based on the consistency of guidance supporting \nincreasing temperatures, and drier air for later this week and \nweekend, an Extreme Heat Warning was issued for the Grand Canyon,\nbelow 4000 feet, starting 10 AM MST Thursday, and then extending \nto Yavapai County valleys and basins below 4000 feet and to Marble\nand Glen Canyons, and Oak Creek and Sycamore Canyons, including \nSedona, starting 10 AM MST/11 AM MDT Friday. These hazards are \nexpected to continue trough 8 PM MST/9PM MDT Sunday.",
